WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.002
-Our first guest this evening is
a talented actress

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:05.972
who you know from her work in
films like "Definitely Maybe,"

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.573
"Hot Rod,"
and "Wedding Crashers."

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.076
She stars in the new series
"Wolf Like Me".

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.612
All six episodes will be
available to stream on Peacock

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:13.413
starting tomorrow.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.714
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:18.217
-♪ Every fortress falls ♪

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:20.820
-Who put on that song?

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.622
-Um, I don't know.

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:25.424
-Did you just put on the song?

00:00:25.424 --> 00:00:28.027
-I've been sitting here
with you.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:29.562
-Um, I need to go.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:30.497
-Did I do something wrong?
I feel like --

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:32.531
-No, I just feel like this is --

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:36.135
um, I shouldn't have put myself
in this situation.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:37.604
-What situation?

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:39.873
-I haven't day drunk
in a really long time, so --

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:41.540
-I don't think it's day anymore.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.843
-Really? What time is it?
-After 6:00.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:47.546
-Huh!
-♪ I don't believe I know you ♪

00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:51.684
♪ Yet, I don't truly
know myself ♪

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:53.486
-Please welcome to the show
Isla Fisher.

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:56.623
How are you, Isla?
-I'm good. But, wait.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.791
I love that you're wearing
a flannel shirt

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:00.526
and I'm dressed up
for the Oscars.

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:05.164
-I know, I feel like we do a bad
job of letting our guests know.

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:08.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.604
This is like we met --
we met on Tinder

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.506
and I did a terrible job
of telling you

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.308
where we were going to go
for our first date.

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.844
-Yes, yes, that is so true.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.745
But I would like --
What's the fancy one?

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:21.848
Isn't it Raya?
We would have met on Raya.

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.582
-Oh, we would have been on Raya.
You're right.

00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:24.784
We would not have been --

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.587
We wouldn't have been
slumming it on Tinder.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.156
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.991
Isla, congratulations
on this show,

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:35.828
which has romance elements,
horror elements,

00:01:35.828 --> 00:01:39.298
and it is almost impossible
to talk about

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.067
without giving something away.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:44.070
-Yeah, it has been really fun
during press for this movie.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.006
It is like the list of talking
points is just two pages

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:48.374
of what I can't say,

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.276
but I will say that

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.278
people really seem to have
connected so far.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.113
We've got pretty good reviews,

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:56.950
which I'm really happy about
because I'm very proud of this.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.219
It is sort of like an
exploration of

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:03.756
how difficult it is
to have a romance or to be --

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.791
to fall in love,
how scary it is,

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:07.627
and sort of
genre-bending element

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:10.096
is just sort of a reminder of

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:13.266
really the danger
of being open to love

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:16.969
and how the baggage we bring
into relationships.

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:20.706
-One thing we can say because we
did officially give it away

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:23.276
in the clip is the Josh Gad
is in this show,

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:25.144
who is incredibly talented...
-He is.

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:26.512
-...and wonderful,
and it must be nice

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.281
to do a show
with somebody like that.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:30.049
-By the way, completely agree,
Seth.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.350
Thank you for highlighting

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.285
the fact that every day onset
was fun.

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:35.421
And as we both know, because
we've been in this business

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:38.157
sadly since "Hot Rod,"
so a very long time,

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:42.895
that often -- that, you know,
those relationships --

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:45.732
Put it this way,
Josh comes to work every day

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:49.134
very happy and
a very supportive scene partner.

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:50.870
I mean, we shot it in Australia
though,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.638
and he was obsessed
with kangaroos

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:55.775
and he just kept saying,
"Isla, where are the kangaroos?"

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.377
I'd be like, "Josh, we're
in Sydney, we're in a city."

00:02:58.377 --> 00:02:59.846
It is not like we're in --

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:02.715
"There are no kangaroos
in a coffee shop, mate.

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:04.350
You have to
just cool your jets."

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.418
But we did have some chickens
and a goat.

00:03:06.418 --> 00:03:10.155
So he saw wildlife. Not
necessarily Australian wildlife.

00:03:10.155 --> 00:03:12.425
-Did he ever --
over the course of his visit,

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:13.926
did you at least show him
one kangaroo?

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:16.495
Did you send him out
to find one?

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:19.399
-He got --
He got sick when he landed.

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:21.033
Actually, the scene
that you just showed,

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:22.668
sort of the kissing scene,

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:25.171
we both had snot
streaming out of our nose,

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.773
so it was not
the most romantic day.

00:03:27.773 --> 00:03:30.343
And I feel like every weekend
he had to do --

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.811
No, he didn't see a kangaroo.

00:03:32.811 --> 00:03:34.646
He didn't even see
a wallaby or a koala.

00:03:34.646 --> 00:03:38.418
I'm pretty sure he -- no.

00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:42.422
You need to ask him, but I let
him down as a tourist guide.

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:43.890
-Yeah.
-I was not there.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.358
-I can't believe you --

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:47.926
the consolation animal you
showed him was a chicken.

00:03:47.926 --> 00:03:50.796
That's really --
I mean, that's really rough.

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:51.964
-Well, that's in the show.

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:55.167
I mean, I didn't even go
out of my way to -- yeah.

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:59.939
-So you --
you and your husband, Sacha,

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.041
you celebrated an anniversary
last month.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:04.810
Is it 20 years?
-Right.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.479
Which in Hollywood standards
is like 200 years.

00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:10.049
Golden jubilee.
It is unheard of.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.717
-Congratulations.

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:14.053
And this is a detail
that I will never get tired

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.155
of talking about
or hearing about.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:19.892
You guys met in clown school.
-Right.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.527
We didn't actually meet
in clown school,

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:24.062
but we did both
go to clown school.

00:04:24.062 --> 00:04:26.365
-Gotcha. So it is a very --
a very good ice breaker

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.501
when you finally met
to talk about clown school.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:29.869
-Right. I mean, listen.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:33.406
You know, I studied
with Jacques Lecoq in Paris.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.374
He studied with Philippe Gaulier
in London.

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.576
My, you know, red nose was born

00:04:37.576 --> 00:04:39.012
in the ninth [indistinct].

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:40.412
His was born in the seventh.

00:04:40.412 --> 00:04:44.817
My clown shoes are a 1'5",
and his are only half a foot.

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.619
So I like to think that --
No, I'm just kidding.

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:49.222
There's not much clown rivalry
at home,

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:51.324
but there are a lot of
conversations

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:53.393
about commedia dell'arte
and bouffon,

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:55.995
and we can get competitive.

00:04:55.995 --> 00:05:00.866
-I can't believe this,
because it was such a --

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:02.968
I mean, it was the first time I
think a lot of people saw you,

00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:04.336
was in "Wedding Crashers."

00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:07.640
It was such a brilliantly funny
performance,

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.010
and yet I recently heard
it was the first comedy role

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.646
you ever auditioned for.
-Yeah.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:15.882
I mean it is weird because
growing up

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:18.517
I had like extra large ears

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:20.519
and I was really short
with red hair in Australia,

00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:22.555
so I did use comedy
to make friends.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:25.691
My parents put me in a different
school every year, so I did --

00:05:25.691 --> 00:05:28.427
I was always kind of a jokester
or prankster I guess.

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:31.229
But professionally I didn't
really know that comedy

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:32.731
was really an option,

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:34.333
even though my dad is like

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:35.968
the funniest person
you would ever meet.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:39.138
But, yes, Sacha was the one
that said, "You're so funny.

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:41.874
You say the funniest jokes.
You should do a comedy."

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:44.210
And then I told my agent,
and actually "Wedding Crashers"

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.545
was the first one
that I auditioned for,

00:05:46.545 --> 00:05:48.081
and the rest is history.

00:05:48.081 --> 00:05:49.515
Yeah, no, I'm lucky. I get to --

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:51.350
Like, "Wolf Like Me"
is very dramatic,

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.085
but I still get to do comedies

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:55.588
which is, you know,
tapping into your inner idiot.

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:58.824
The most fun you can ever have.
-I agree.

00:05:58.824 --> 00:06:01.527
I have a lot of questions about
some other stuff

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:02.995
including your work
as an author.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:06.199
We will be back with more
Isla Fisher right after this.

